narrative:
## A Time-Aware Greeting Bot

A great greeting changes with context. With `if/elif/else`, your function can pick the right greeting for any time of day.

### Time-Based Greetings

```xiom
fn greet(name: Str, hour: Int) -> Str {
  if hour >= 5 && hour < 12 {
    return "Good morning, " + name + "!";
  } else if hour >= 12 && hour < 17 {
    return "Good afternoon, " + name + "!";
  } else if hour >= 17 && hour < 21 {
    return "Good evening, " + name + "!";
  } else {
    return "Hello, " + name + " — it's late!";
  }
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(greet("Kilo", 9));   // Good morning, Kilo!
  io.println(greet("Kilo", 14));  // Good afternoon, Kilo!
  io.println(greet("Kilo", 19));  // Good evening, Kilo!
  io.println(greet("Kilo", 23));  // Hello, Kilo — it's late!
  return 0;
}
```

### Adding Formality

```xiom
fn greet_formal(title: Str, last_name: Str, hour: Int) -> Str {
  let full_name = title + " " + last_name;
  if hour >= 5 && hour < 12 {
    return "Good morning, " + full_name + ".";
  } else if hour >= 12 && hour < 17 {
    return "Good afternoon, " + full_name + ".";
  } else {
    return "Good evening, " + full_name + ".";
  }
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(greet_formal("Dr.", "Patel", 10));
  return 0;
}
```

### How It Works

The function checks ranges of time using `&&` (AND):
- Morning: hour is 5 or later AND before 12
- Afternoon: hour is 12 or later AND before 17
- Evening: hour is 17 or later AND before 21
- Night: anything else (the `else` catch-all)

Using `&&` combines two conditions: `hour >= 5 && hour < 12` means hour must be at least 5 AND less than 12. Both must be true.

